WEBVTT

00:00:08,780 --> 00:00:14,520
Okay, on your site, you advise fasting prior to having chemotherapy administered certain

00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:19,460
chemo, such as full-fox, required fusions that go across the period of 48 hours.

00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:24,900
What do you recommend, what do you recommend, recommendations for fasting given that treatment?

00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:30,420
Also, when I asked the clinic advised against fasting, as in lower blood sugars, it may

00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:35,850
result in nausea during treatment.

00:00:35,850 --> 00:00:46,330
Well, there's multiple studies of fasting in chemotherapy, and one of them, one of them

00:00:46,330 --> 00:00:54,250
tested people fasting for 48 hours prior to the chemotherapy infusion, and another

00:00:54,250 --> 00:00:58,650
fasted for 24 hours prior to the chemotherapy infusion.

00:00:58,650 --> 00:01:04,610
And I know there's other practitioners that ask people to fast 24 hours before their infusion

00:01:04,610 --> 00:01:09,610
in 24 hours after the initial intake of the infusion, so you wouldn't need to tell

00:01:09,610 --> 00:01:13,850
you got home maybe 18 hours after you got home.

00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:20,450
I've always asked people to cut it in the middle and just do a 24 hour fast prior to the

00:01:20,450 --> 00:01:24,410
chemotherapy infusion and eat when you get home.

00:01:24,410 --> 00:01:31,010
Now if you're doing the chemotherapy, the whole idea of that basically is to lower your blood

00:01:31,010 --> 00:01:34,330
sugar before you do chemotherapy.

00:01:34,330 --> 00:01:40,570
Basically, if you could think of it as a cartoon, the cancer cells are much hungrier than

00:01:40,570 --> 00:01:45,010
your regular cells because they're more metabolically active.

00:01:45,010 --> 00:01:50,010
Because they're in a state of rapid replication, there's a lot more going on in a shorter period

00:01:50,010 --> 00:01:54,250
of time inside of a cancer cell, so they're burning a lot more energy, and they're burning

00:01:54,250 --> 00:02:02,570
energy very inefficiently, not through the crem cycle primarily, but through the end point

00:02:02,570 --> 00:02:10,290
of glycolysis, which is an inefficient way to make energy because the cells are that

00:02:10,290 --> 00:02:13,730
is healthy as a normal cell.

00:02:13,730 --> 00:02:18,130
So they need a lot of glucose, so they're gobbling up glucose.

00:02:18,130 --> 00:02:27,490
So if you starve them for a 24 hours prior to chemotherapy, if you could think of it as

00:02:27,490 --> 00:02:36,690
they're super hungry, they're going to uptake chemotherapy more than a healthy cell will,

00:02:36,690 --> 00:02:40,530
and that is kind of the theory behind fasting.

00:02:40,530 --> 00:02:47,810
Now all the research, whether they were doing 48 hours prior to chemo, fasting 24 hours,

00:02:47,810 --> 00:02:55,290
24 hours after, or just 24 hours before, all the research showed a decrease in symptoms

00:02:55,290 --> 00:03:02,090
by all the people that did it, a decrease in side effects of the chemotherapy, and though

00:03:02,090 --> 00:03:09,570
you can't prove that they had better results because there's too many factors involved, I

00:03:09,570 --> 00:03:13,970
think you get better results because the chemos get a better chance of uptaking the

00:03:13,970 --> 00:03:14,970
cancer cells.

00:03:14,970 --> 00:03:21,090
So of course, if it's making you sick or something like that, you have to do what is going

00:03:21,090 --> 00:03:27,050
to be best for you, there's not a perfect number or anything like that, but I think fasting

00:03:27,050 --> 00:03:32,490
for a period of time, however you feel comfortable fasting prior to that infusion, it's going

00:03:32,490 --> 00:03:33,490
to be better.

00:03:33,490 --> 00:03:38,010
What do you do when you got 48 hour period of the infusion itself, well you're probably

00:03:38,010 --> 00:03:43,810
going to have to eat something during that time, but again, eating small amount during

00:03:43,810 --> 00:03:50,450
that time is not going to give this giant fuel source to the cancer cells.

00:03:50,450 --> 00:03:59,570
So you just have to do what you can do, and but I think fasting prior to the initial infusion

00:03:59,570 --> 00:04:04,400
is going to be your best bet.

00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:14,960
So this clinic is one doing 50 gram IV vitamin C infusions immediately after the oxaloplac.

00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:23,540
I am always advised to have food with the IV vitamin C, which further complicates fasting

00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:28,760
due to the IV vitamin C, also lowering blood sugars, or so I understand.

00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:35,200
So if they're doing it right after, I mean, again, if you did the 24 hour fast, you got

00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:43,400
the IV of the chemo, and then you're going to do the IV infusion of the vitamin C, you could

00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:46,600
be eating while you do that, I mean, that's just one thought.

